WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.700
Snake's Revenge.

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:06.880
The following commands may be ignored by cowards, traitors, and enemy spies.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:30.620
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.760
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:34.500
And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:42.400
Guys, you may be noticing that I didn't say Metal Gear at the top, and that is because the box omits that.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:50.120
This is clearly a sequel to Metal Gear, but it's not called Metal Gear 2 Snake's Revenge.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.040
It's just Snake's Revenge.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:01:01.500
Yeah, I learned pretty close to, you know, just starting the game that this is not the game that I thought it was.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:10.400
And I knew this game existed just from being a fan of the Metal Gear series in general, but I guess I'm not that big of a fan.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:17.500
Because I could have swore that we were playing, like, the canonical second game, and I was very disappointed.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.280
So this is, so the other NES games are canon, and this is not.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.780
What do you mean by other NES games?

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.260
So there's just Metal Gear, and then Snake's Revenge on the NES.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.960
Oh, yeah. Whereas the... When you referenced, you thought this was another game, I thought maybe there was an NES.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:40.340
So, yeah, there's another Kojima sequel to Metal Gear called Metal Gear 2 Solid Snake.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.860
And I thought that this was just a different name for the same game, and it's not that.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.700
But Metal Gear 2 Solid Snake is not on the NES?

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:49.820
It is not.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.720
It was made just for the MSX 2.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:59.740
And as Sean mentioned, Hideo Kojima is not involved with this port, Snake's Revenge, at all.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.780
The NES version of Metal Gear is a little different than the MSX version of Metal Gear.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:14.580
So I guess, like, if you actually had a canon, the Metal Gear MSX version would be the one to play, not necessarily the NES version.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.440
But that said, at least Kojima was involved in that one.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.720
For this Snake's Revenge, he was not involved at all.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:27.220
In fact, it was being developed, like, almost simultaneously with Metal Gear 2 Solid Snake.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.200
So that's kind of funny.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:29.960
But, you know, it's Konami.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.940
They own the game, so they don't have to keep bringing the same person to make them.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.700
It's like, did Miyamoto make every single Mario game?

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.500
No, but he probably saw them, at least.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:38.760
And that's what's the difference here.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:45.140
Kojima did not really get even a chance to say, well, maybe I wouldn't make it a side-scrolling game.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.400
Like, focus on the self.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.860
Yeah, at that point, like, he wasn't Kojima.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.340
Kojima. He was just some guy named Kojima that made the first one.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:03:03.380
And, like, he wasn't this titan that should have a hand creatively and everything.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.640
Yeah, he was just like some guy in the payroll.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.800
Absolutely, Sean. He also made Antarctic Adventure, which you'll remember we played on a Bytes episode.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.060
And we were all like, what?

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:13.040
Why are we doing this?

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.760
Like, there's nothing special about this game.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.600
And it was because Kojima made it.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:17.560
It was his first game.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.480
So from penguins to stealth games to no involvement whatsoever.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:27.160
This sequel was produced specifically for the Western market.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:32.560
And if anybody's wondering what Kojima's thoughts are on the game, it's a little bit of like a ping pong.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.100
First, he's like, yeah, it's fine.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.220
It's faithful to the whole Metal Gear concept.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:42.980
Then, like, in 2009 at GDC, he was like, this is a crappy game.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:52.360
And then in another interview with Nintendo Power back when they were still around but after the 2009 GDC, he said that he doesn't consider it to be a bad game.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.540
So it went from it's good, it's bad.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.280
It's not bad, but it's not good.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.280
Now, this last interview, was this pre or post Konami blow up?

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.420
Uh, pre. Okay.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.180
So we need another interview with him now.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.180
Exactly. He probably would have been working on 5 at the time.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.920
But wasn't he not involved in one of them?

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.720
Before? Wasn't 5 the one after the blow up?

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.700
It released after, but...

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.680
Oh, okay. Isn't like Metal Gear Online the one he's not involved in?

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:21.940
I think so.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.260
But I know that Metal Gear 5 came out after every time.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:30.400
Everything happened, but he still, like, very much was the developer of the game.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:34.480
Which is the one where you, like, attach people to balloons and shoot them into the air?

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.120
That is 5. Okay.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.480
Right, well, that sounds like a Kojima game to me.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.080
He made a whole game where you just walk around.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:44.840
And attach people to balloons.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.380
My, um, my...

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:53.260
Maybe you answered this already, but just to clarify, so is Metal Gear 1 canon still?

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:57.060
With the Metal Gear Solid stuff on the PlayStation 1 and beyond?

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:03.440
Yeah. Okay. I wasn't sure if, like, all the NES games happened and then they were like, okay, now we're gonna make the...

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.760
You know, they, like, rebooted it on the PlayStation 1 or whatever.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:15.080
No, no. In fact, what's really cool, and, um, you know, we should probably do, like, a Metal Gear Solid bonus episode at some time, because I just played it and it's one of Sean's favorite games.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:25.800
Um, what's cool is that they have, like, you can get a bunch of codecs before you ever even jump into the game of listening about the events of Metal Gear 1 and 2.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.140
But specifically the adventures on the MSX.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:32.160
That said, they weren't that different between the NES and MSX for the first Metal Gear.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:46.640
And, uh, in, I forgot if it's, like, Substance or Subsistence, one of those, like, re-release games, um, I think they have both, like, all of the 8-bit, um, Metal Gear's playable.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:50.740
I think even both, like, Snake's Revenge and Solid Snake.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:54.060
Um, so it's, it's kind of funny.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:58.060
Uh, so they kind of treat them all as, as one big happy family.

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:09.020
Anyway. So, all this talk about Metal Gear as a franchise and everything, now we have to remember that, uh, we don't have to judge this based on, uh, future games.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:19.140
Like, don't worry about Metal Gear Solid or Metal Gear Solid 5 with balloons or whatever Kojima winds up doing after Death Stranding, which is apparently just Death Stranding 2.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:21.140
Uh, forget all that for a second.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:26.600
And let's just talk about Snake's Revenge as a follow-up to the Metal Gear we played on the NES.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:30.280
Is it a noticeable improvement over the first Metal Gear?

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:42.220
Were there things that you guys saw in your gameplay that was like, well, I'm happy they added this, or, you know, oh, they really, uh, made the stealth, like, more intuitive or complex or something?

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:55.620
I don't fully remember, like, a lot of specifics about Metal Gear, other than the fact that I found it interesting and I enjoyed it and, and it, and I remember it being, like, not perfect.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:07:03.380
But this one, I did feel, from what I remember, I felt much more frustrated in this one more often.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:15.020
So, like, again, so, like, I don't have, like, specifics, but, like, based on what I was, maybe my expectations were high because I remembered liking the original, but I remember finding this one more frustrating for, for various reasons.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:32.180
I think, uh, I remember, like, one of the key differences, and, again, my, my memory of the first one is fuzzy, but I, I feel like we, we harped a bit about, like, if you change screens, then, like, your, like, the alarm stops.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:40.560
Like, is that, was that a thing, like, if you were to trip an alarm and then you went to a new screen, would the alarm still be going in Metal Gear?

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:41.940
I believe you're correct.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.040
So, it wouldn't, right?

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.640
It wouldn't, yeah, it was like, oh, it was a glitch out, essentially.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:08:00.560
So, the fact that this had that continuity, I thought was an improvement, at least in, like, oh, I'm a spy, and, like, I don't just have to walk over, uh, 15 feet and everyone forgets about me.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.920
It's like, no, you gotta, like, clear out the room.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.020
Yeah, and then, once you've killed three people, they're like, oh, I guess there was probably no danger ever.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:14.240
Well, you just killed everyone, you killed everyone that heard you, is how I, like, headcanoned.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:16.880
Yeah, but what about the alarm that was going off in the whole building?

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:21.120
Like, the alarm is for us, it's, it's an alarm in your head.

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:22.520
I get it.

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:33.180
Well, what's, what's weird is, is you can track the alarm across multiple screens, but they still couldn't get the idea that, like, enemies you killed on the screen before are dead.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:39.980
Well, yeah, I mean, that's, I think that's also a balance thing, because it's, they're very easy to kill.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:46.940
Yeah, you spend a lot of time running through, back and forth through different things, so I feel like, pretty quickly, just the whole building would be empty.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:48.880
Yeah, so I get that.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:55.340
I think what they could have done as a trade-off, though, is, because this is gonna be one of the things I complain about, so I'm just gonna spend a little more time on it.

00:08:55.340 --> 00:09:16.900
One of the things they could have done as a trade-off is, like, after X amount of screens, enemies come back, because it's, it's not necessarily designed in a way, because it happens many times in the game, where you will enter the screen, and it will have had the enemies designed in a way that they will not immediately see you, and so you'll be able to be like, okay, well, what's the stealth route here?

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:23.700
Like, do I go behind these barrels and then punch this guy, or, you know, do I have the silencer so I don't have to worry about being as stealthy?

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:24.820
And that's all fine.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:41.020
And then you're gonna go through the door that you needed the key card for, or whatever, and you go through that door, and you get that new item, and then you come out of that door, and the enemies were never designed as if you were gonna come out of two different entrances, and so they spot you right away, and it's like, well, this is dumb.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:42.940
Like, now I don't even get the chance to be stealthy.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:52.760
That seems to be how everything works, like, because even if you were to enter a room that has pickups in it, and then walk back out, and then walk back into that room, the pickups are there.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:59.180
So you can, like, sort of cheese that, and I guess the trade-off is that the enemies get to kind of cheese you.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:04.740
Um, and it sounds, it feels like, you know, it was a design oversight, and less, less a bug.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:17.540
Yeah, you know, I actually, the more I'm sitting with what I just said, like, in defense of that, the more I'm not sure I agree with it anymore, because I don't think it necessarily would be such a bad thing that you clear out the whole building.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:19.300
Because, like, you did that.

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:25.980
You earned that. Maybe you gotta make the enemies a little harder to sneak past for that to work, so then it feels, like, a little more weighty.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:33.380
But, like, you know, you think of a modern game, and it might be silly to, like, compare it, but, like, you know, you think, not even modern, you know, like, you think of, like, Dishonored 2.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:37.260
Which is what, that's, like, six years old now, or if not more.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:43.040
But, still, like, you think of something like that, where it's like, yeah, you kill everybody in this building, and then they're all dead, they don't come back.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:51.520
That doesn't ruin my experience, it actually makes it, like, okay, like, I've earned now the ability to walk a little more freely, because I've stealthily took out enough people.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:54.520
You know, and I think that you can design that properly here.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:11:03.920
Because, yeah, what you said, Mike, actually does strike me as a big problem, even if it's not, like, oh, I walked back out of this room I had to go into and immediately was caught, which, that's a problem in itself.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:09.360
But also, it's like, I just put a lot of, like, energy into being as stealthy as possible.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:11.060
It's like, okay, now I just gotta, like, do it again.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:12.380
Like, exactly the same.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:14.900
Like, it just doesn't, it starts to lose its, uh...

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:16.420
I don't know if it's a problem.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.680
I can understand not liking it.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:25.940
But I don't think that that's, like, the wrong way to go about it.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.220
It's a stealth game. You're supposed to just not be seen.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:33.780
I think, in a way, it's sort of disincentivizing you of trying to, like, go and just kill everybody.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:36.060
Because it's really all for naught.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:37.960
So just try not to be seen.

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:40.940
It'll be a lot less of a headache in the end.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:45.780
I don't know. Yeah, I mean, I wasn't also saying that, like, it's the wrong way to go about it.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:53.100
I guess I was just saying that I don't think what I said initially about clearing everybody out is wrong either.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:54.740
You know, I think that that could totally work.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:12:02.520
I think I'm just looking for more harmony between the be stealthy and punishment for getting caught.

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:08.620
Because it is actually, you know, tough in a game where you don't have that much health.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:10.460
And, yeah, you can cheese the rations.

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:13.840
But if you're not near them, then you'd have to backtrack back to them if you run out.

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:21.280
But it is punishing to be caught and to have, like, multiple enemies following you as the alarm is triggered.

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:24.260
And so you're rewarded for being stealthy.

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:29.500
Like, this is the kind of game that you theoretically don't ever want to be caught in.

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:38.020
And that's impossible in this version just because certain pickups require certain entrances that just weren't designed around.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:39.220
And so you will be seen.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:45.200
And so it doesn't feel as cool because it's like, oh, this would have been a great game if it could be completed 100% pacifist.

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:51.300
But because it relies so much on the stealth, I was surprised to see that there are some situations you just can't avoid.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:59.900
I guess I don't remember any that were, like, I was auto-spotted while getting out of a room or anything.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:01.740
But I'll take your word for it.

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:05.300
Yeah, I remember that happening, like, once or twice, but, like, very rare.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:11.800
Right. It's mostly on those, like, when you actually enter a room, not necessarily from screen to screen,

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:19.520
which is what makes me think that they designed them from entering from a different hallway than from entering the rooms.

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:22.580
Because the rooms are purposefully being guarded, right?

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:24.920
Like, the enemy focus, attention is there.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:28.120
So they should have just made it so that if you're coming out of said room,

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:31.080
that the enemies are now looking in the hallways first or something.

00:13:54.840 --> 00:14:01.020
But the actual biggest change in terms of gameplay is this side-scrolling addition.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:05.920
In the first game, you were ever only playing top-down stealth gameplay.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:10.500
And in this one, they've kind of added a whole new mode, essentially,

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:20.560
where you're snake in, like, the same way that Link was side-scrolling in The Adventures of Link.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:24.400
It's like, oh, I didn't think Zelda games ever looked this kind of way.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.760
It's like, oh, I didn't think Metal Gear games ever functioned in this way.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:30.500
It's not that it's terrible or anything.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:33.340
It's just a little bit different from...

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:37.920
It makes the game almost like Contra and less like Metal Gear.

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:40.240
Yeah, I am...

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:46.640
You know, and the people that I was watching after playing through it myself,

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:51.740
I got a lot of Russian attack comparisons,

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:54.480
which I guess I can kind of feel.

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:58.600
I remember a bit of Russian attack and the vibe that that had.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:05.320
I see that you can finally go prone, which is a Metal Gear staple.

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:10.600
Laying down and then moving that way, that's always fun,

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:11.940
sort of shimmying on your belly.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.680
Like, who doesn't do that when you're playing Metal Gear?

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:18.980
And, you know, there's the underwater sections,

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:21.740
which seem kind of frustrating and terrible.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:26.380
But those are the kinds of things that I saw in the side-scrolling.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:29.660
It is strange because it's side-scrolling,

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:32.800
but I still feel the DNA of it being a stealth game.

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:36.400
It doesn't feel like it's really...

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:40.340
Like, it's very, like, fun to move specifically.

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:43.420
It doesn't feel like there's a lot of great, like, motion physics.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:47.380
But there is opportunity in this side-scroller

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:49.900
to, like, duck under someone's line of view

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:51.260
and take them out with a knife.

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:54.460
Or, like, yeah, like, sneak through the water

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:58.040
and come up behind someone and shoot with a silencer or whatever.

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:00.840
Like, so, like, that is just interesting.

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:02.060
Because usually when you think side-scroller,

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:06.340
you think it is, like, physics or, like, action-based.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:07.500
And this is still...

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.180
Seems to be trying to...

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:10.800
Either it's trying to keep some stealth

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:12.940
or it did a bad job of being an action game.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:14.860
But, like, I felt like it was...

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:16.860
It had, like, stealth DNA.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.760
Yeah, it's very procedural in the sense of, like,

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:24.760
you only see enough information to think about your upcoming tasks.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:30.820
So whether it's another soldier or it's the water or it's platforms,

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:34.760
it's, like, you're being very methodical about your next steps

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.160
and using the appropriate equipment.

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:38.980
Like, you might change between the gun and the knife

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:42.880
or, you know, like, making sure you have the oxygen tank for the water.

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:44.780
So I like that.

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:47.000
And then, Sean, I liked your Russian Attack mention

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:48.180
because that's a Konami game.

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:50.240
So who knows?

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:53.460
Like, maybe there was a little bit of the same team working on this.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:55.360
Yeah, like, how that worked?

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:56.440
Let's do that.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:58.020
We don't need this Hideo guy.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:01.060
Also, in terms of equipment, you know,

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:02.920
we mentioned the oxygen tank for water,

